No, raising the pool size would only delay the problem. `withClient` in `db.js` leaks a connection whenever `fn` throws, because `client.release()` on line 14 is skipped and nothing catches the error.

The log fits that. `idle=0` and `total=20` never change, while `waiting` climbs from 7 to 28. A pool that was just undersized would show `waiting` rise and fall as load changes.

The fix is to release in a `finally`:

```js
async function withClient(fn) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    return await fn(client);
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

A leak can also come from callers that use `pool.connect()` directly and never release, and I haven't checked for those. `pool.log` also covers only 14:02 to 14:04, not the last hour, so I can't tell from it when the exhaustion began.

